<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign">
	<terms:abstract xml:lang="en">The System Design(SD) Ontology is designed to encapsulate the essential elements of system design, particularly within aerospace applications. It integrates Basic Formal Ontology (BFO) and IOF-Core to provide a basis for modeling the technical, functional, and operational aspects of complex systems. SD ontology defines terms related to system components, functions, and processes, supporting both decomposition of systems into sub-systems and modeling interactions such as material and control flows. Its amis to ensure a common understanding in system design such that multidisciplinary cooperation could be improved.</terms:abstract>
	<terms:creator xml:lang="en">Zhengyu Liu, University of Technology of Tarbes</terms:creator>
	<terms:publisher xml:lang="fr">Laboratoire Genie Production,UNIVERSITÉ DE TECHNOLOGIE TARBES OCCITANIE PYRÉNÉES</terms:publisher>
	<terms:title xml:lang="en">System Design Ontology (SysDO)</terms:title>
	<owl:imports rdf:resource="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/202401/core/Core/"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#Port">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">A component C has two ports, p1 and p2. Through p1, C receives input data . After processing, C sends the output data through p2 to the next component in the system.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#PortFunction">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">Basic function of a port can be either sending or receiving data as specified in its subclasses</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlEndProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The control end process e, where the input is a control flow x. Once x reaches e, the process halts, with no further actions or activities in the system beyond this point.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlow">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">Control flow often trigger state changes or actions in the receiving components. sometimes they are represented as special types of information flows, but they are distinguished by their purpose of influencing system behavior rather than just transferring data.</skos:example>
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">signal from central controller to end actuator</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#InformationFlow">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The rolling speed of wheels are captured to sensors and presented on screen</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowDecisionProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The control flow decision process d, where the input is a control flow f. If the condition evaluated at d is true, the flow is directed to path p1; if false, it is directed to path p2.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowForkProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The fork process f, where the input is a single flow x. At f, the flow x is split into two parallel flows, x1 and x2, which are executed simultaneously.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowJoinProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The control flow joint process j, where the inputs are two control flows x1 and x2. Once both x1 and x2 complete, they are merged into a single flow y that is the output of j.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowMergeProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The merge process m, where the inputs are flows a, b, and c. When any one of these flows is activated, the process continues with a single flow d, not necessarily waiting for all inputs to complete.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlStartProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The start process s, which has no input flow. Once triggered, it generates an output flow x that initiates the execution of the all the subsequent activities in the system.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlTransferProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The transfer process t, where control flow x is passed from component a to component b. Once a completes its execution, control flow x is handed over to b for further processing.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#Flow">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In a car manufacturing plant design model, the designed movement of car bodies along the assembly line from one workstation to another</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FlowSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">A manual of drink production line the movement of bottles through the production line, detailing the rate of flow, the routing between stations</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ReceivingFunctionSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">Receiving function of port p1 accepts only pdf file</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SendingFunctionSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">sending function of port p1 send only int data</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FunctionFlowSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In a function flow specification FS1, flow f1 is designed to start by  the sending function A  via port p1</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FunctionPrecedenceSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">function f1 f2 f3 must be triggered respectively, that is f1 precedes f2 that precedes f3</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SystemFunction">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">the Anti-lock braking system (ABS) of a car's function is to prevent wheel lockup during hard braking</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FunctionSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">A manual in car manufacturing plant on the series of welding operations required to assemble the car body</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowDecisionProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In the material flow decision process p1, material M is at location L1. The system evaluates whether the material meets a specific condition, for example weight. If the condition is satisfied, the material is directed to location L2; if not, it is sent to location L3.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowForkProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In the material flow fork process p1, material M at location L1 is split into two concurrent flows. One part of M, m1 is sent to location L2 for processing, while another part m2 is simultaneously sent to location L3. Both flows occur in parallel, enabling multiple operations to be carried out on the material.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowJoinProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In the material flow joint process p1, materials M1 from location L1 and M2 from location L2 are combined into a single flow at location L3. The process waits for both M1 and M2 to arrive at L3 before proceeding</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowMergeProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In the material flow merge process p1, materials M1 from location L1, M2 from location L2, and M3 from location L3 are merged into a single flow at location L4. As soon as any one of M1, M2, or M3 arrives at L4 the process continues without waiting for all materials to arrive.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialSinkProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The material sink process p1, where the input is a material flow x. Once x reaches e, the material location change process halts, with no further actions or activities in the system beyond this point.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialSourceProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">The source process p1, which has no input flow. Once triggered, it generates an output material location change flow x that initiates the execution of the all the subsequent activities in the system.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialTransferProcess">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In the material transfer process p1, material M is transferred from station S1to station S2.</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SystemFlowSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In a system flow specification SS1, the flow f1 starts at the outgoing port p1 of system X</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SystemPrecedenceSpecification">
	<skos:example xml:lang="en">In a system precedence specification SS1, system s1 must complete its processing before system s2 can initiate. that is s1 precedes s2</skos:example>
</rdf:Description>

