<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#handlesMaterial">
	<skos:definition xml:lang="en">x handles material y = there exists process boudary z, port x participates in process boundary z, and material entity y participates in process boundary z.</skos:definition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasLogicalSubSystemAtSomeTime">
	<skos:definition>a has logical subsystem b = a, b are both logical systems, and logical system b is a part of logical system a that make its logic complet</skos:definition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasSubSystemAtSomeTime">
	<skos:definition>a has subsystem b = a, b are both systems, and system b is a part of system a that make it work</skos:definition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#PrecedenceSpecification">
	<skos:definition xml:lang="en">Restrictions indicating that the installation of the dashboard must occur after the windshield is in place but before the seats are installed</skos:definition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowJoinProcess">
	<explanatoryNote xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">typically waiting for all incoming flows to complete before proceeding to the combined flow.</explanatoryNote>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#Flow">
	<explanatoryNote xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">Flows describe how different parts of a system interact and how resources or information move through the system. In SysML, flows are often represented using Item Flows on connectors between blocks.</explanatoryNote>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#handlesMaterial">
	<firstOrderLogicAxiom xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/">handleMaterial(x,y) ↔ Port(x) ∧ MaterialEntity(y) ∧ ∃z(ProcessBoundary(z) participatesIn(x,z) ∧ participatesIn(y,z)</firstOrderLogicAxiom>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#Port">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A port is a interaction point through which data, command, material, or energy can enter or leave the owning block.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasFunctionPartAtAllTimes">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">p has function part c at all times =Def for all times t, p exists at t implies (p is a function, c is a function, and c functions to make p functions some way at t)</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasFunctionPartAtSomeTime">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a has function part b = a, b are both functions, and function b make function a works</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasInputState">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">process a has input state b, means b is the process boundary at the input of a</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasOutputState">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">process a has output state b, means b is the process boundary at the output of a</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasAmountOfWorkers">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/">'has amount of workers' notifies the the amount of workers or labors is required in a specific process</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#hasDuration">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/">'has duration' is the temporal duration for a planned process, whereas its unit has to be declared such as second or minute</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#PortFunction">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">The function of a port</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlEndProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A  control flow process that terminates the execution or deactivates ae activity, or sequence within a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlow">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A control flow process represents the transfer of control signals or commands that influence the behavior or state of components within a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#InformationFlow">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">An information flow represents the transfer of data, commande, or knowledge between components or actors in a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowDecisionProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A control flow process that evaluates a condition and directs the control flow to one of several possible paths based on the outcome of the option</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowForkProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A control flow process that splits a single control flow into multiple concurrent flows, allowing parallel execution of activities or processes</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowJoinProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A control flow process that combines multiple control flows into a single flow</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlFlowMergeProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A control flow process that combines multiple control flows into a single flow, allowing the process to continue when any one of the incoming flows is activated</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlStartProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A  control flow process that initiates the execution or activation of activity, or sequence within a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ControlTransferProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A control flow process that transfers control from one component or activity to another within a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#Flow">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A connector represents the transfer or exchange of elements (such as material, information, or control signals) between components or parts of a system.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FlowSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a plan specification that prescribes the transfer of materials, information, or energy between different functions or components within a system or process</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ReceivingFunctionSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A function specification that prescribes one or a set of functions or activities about taking something from the exterior.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SendingFunctionSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A function specification is an information content entity that prescribes one or a set of functions or activities about giving something to exterior.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FunctionFlowSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a flow specification of function or functions</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FunctionPrecedenceSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a precedence specification that prescribes the properties and relationships between functions or activities within a function</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#PrecedenceSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a plan specification that prescribes the sequential or parallel relationships between functions or activities within a process or system, including dependencies and timing constraints</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SystemFunction">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">function of a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#FunctionSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a plan specification that prescribes a set of functions or activities to be performed within a system or process.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#IncomingPort">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">An incoming port is an interaction point through which data, command, material, or energy can enter the owning block.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#ReceivingFunction">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">the function of a port to accept incoming data, signals, or control flows from exterior</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowDecisionProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that evaluates a condition and directs the control flow to one of several possible paths based on the outcome of the option</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowForkProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that splits a single control flow into multiple concurrent flows, allowing parallel execution of activities or processes</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowJoinProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that combines multiple control flows into a single flow, typically waiting for all incoming flows to complete before proceeding</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialFlowMergeProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that combines multiple control flows into a single flow, allowing the process to continue when any one of the incoming flows is activated</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialSinkProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that receives, consumes, or removes material from a system, acting as the termination point for a material sequence</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialSourceProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that introduces or generates material into a system, acting as the origin point for a material sequence</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#MaterialTransferProcess">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">A material location change process that transfers control from one component or activity to another within a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#OutgoingPort">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">An incoming port is an interaction point through which data, command, material, or energy can enter the owning block.</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SendingFunction">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/">the function of a port to sending outgouing data, signals, or control flows to exterior</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SystemFlowSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a flow specification of system or systems</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign#SystemPrecedenceSpecification">
	<naturalLanguageDefinition xmlns="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/annotation/" xml:lang="en">a precedence specification that prescribes the properties and relationships between systems or activities within a system</naturalLanguageDefinition>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.pics.lgp.uttop.fr/ontologies/systemdesign">
	<dc:contributor xml:lang="en">Arkopaul Sarkar, University of Technology of Tarbes</dc:contributor>
